ly to need so it does not have to fetch from Random Access Memory (RAM)"},{term:"Register",definition:"Tiny, high-speed storage locations inside the CPU that hold data currently being processed",example:"Memory Address Register (MAR), Memory Data Register (MDR), Accumulator (ACC), Program Counter (PC)"},{term:"Memory Address Register (MAR)",definition:"A register that holds the memory address of the data or instruction the CPU wants to read or write",example:"Before fetching the next instruction, the Program Counter copies its address into the MAR"},{term:"Memory Data Register (MDR)",definition:"A register that holds the data or instruction just fetched from memory, or about to be written to memory",example:"After fetching, the instruction at the address held in the MAR is loaded into the MDR"},{term:"Program Counter (PC)",definition:"A register that holds the memory address of the next instruction to be fetched, incrementing automatically after each fetch",example:"After fetching the instruction at address 100, the PC updates to 101 ready for the next cycle"},{term:"Accumulator (ACC)",definition:"A register inside the ALU that temporarily stores the result of arithmetic and logical operations",example:"When the ALU calculates 5 + 3, the result 8 is held in the Accumulator before being moved to memory"},{term:"Fetch-Decode-Execute (FDE) Cycle",definition:"The three-step process the CPU repeats continuously to carry out every instruction",example:"1. PC holds the address of the next instruction. 2. Address copied to MAR, instruction fetched into MDR. 3. CU decodes it, ALU executes it"},{term:"Clock Speed",definition:"The number of cycles per second the CPU can perform, measured in Gigahertz (GHz)",example:"A 3.5 GHz processor performs 3.5 billion cycles per second"},{term:"Cores",definition:"Independent processing units within a single CPU, each capable of handling its own instructions",example:"A quad-core CPU can process 4 tasks (threads) simultaneously"},{term:"Cache Size",definition:"The amount of cache memory available; more cache means fewer trips to slower RAM",example:"Upgrading from 4 Megabytes (MB) to 8 MB of cache reduces delays"},{term:"Embedded System",definition:"A computer built into a larger device, designed for a single dedicated purpose",example:"Washing machine controller, traffic light system, smart thermostat"},{term:"Von Neumann Architecture",definition:"A model of computer design where a single memory stores both data and instructions, and the CPU processes them sequentially via the fetch-decode-execute cycle",example:"The CPU fetches instructions from the same memory where data is stored, using registers like MAR, MDR, PC and ACC to manage the process"}],"1.2":[{term:"Random Access Memory (RAM)",definition:"Volatile primary storage that holds data and programs currently in use; contents are lost when power is off",example:"When you open Google Chrome, its program code is loaded from storage into RAM"},{term:"Read Only Memory (ROM)",definition:"Non-volatile primary storage that holds the boot-up instructions; contents are permanent",example:"The Basic Input/Output System (BIOS) is stored on ROM and runs when you turn the computer on"},{term:"Virtual Memory",definition:"A section of the hard drive used as temporary RAM when physical RAM is full",example:"When RAM is at capacity, the Operating System (OS) swaps idle programs to disk to free up space"},{term:"Magnetic Storage",definition:"Stores data using magnetised particles on a spinning platter read by a moving arm",example:"Hard Disk Drive (HDD) inside a desktop computer"},{term:"Optical Storage",definition:"Uses a laser to read and write data on a reflective disc",example:"Compact Disc (CD), Digital Versatile Disc (DVD), Blu-ray"},{term:"Solid State Storage",definition:"Uses electronic flash memory with no moving parts, making it faster and more durable",example:"Solid State Drive (SSD), Universal Serial Bus (USB) flash drive"},{term:"Bit",definition:"The smallest unit of data in computing; a single binary digit, either 0 or 1",example:"One switch that is either on (1) or off (0)"},{term:"Nibble",definition:"A group of 4 bits",example:"Half a byte, e.g. 1010"},{term:"Byte",definition:"A group of 8 bits",example:"Can represent a whole number from 0 to 255"},{term:"Kilobyte (KB)",definition:"A unit of data storage equal to about 1000 bytes, or 1024 bytes when using binary units",example:"A small text file might be 50 KB"},{term:"Megabyte (MB)",definition:"A unit of data storage equal to about 1000 kilobytes, or 1024 kilobytes when using binary units",example:"A photo might be several MB"},{term:"Gigabyte (GB)",definition:"A unit of data storage equal to about 1000 megabytes, or 1024 megabytes when using binary units",example:"A high-definition film might be 4 GB"},{term:"Terabyte (TB)",definition:"A unit of data storage equal to about 1000 gigabytes, or 1024 gigabytes when using binary units",example:"A laptop hard drive might store 1 TB of data"},{term:"Petabyte (PB)",definition:"A unit of data storage equal to about 1000 terabytes, or 1024 terabytes when using binary units",example:"Large cloud providers may store many PB of data"},{term:"Binary",definition:"Base-2 number system using only the digits 0 and 1",example:"01001010 in binary represents 74 in denary"},{term:"Denary",definition:"Base-10 number system using digits 0 to 9; the everyday counting system",example:"The number 255 in denary is 11111111 in binary"},{term:"Hexadecimal",definition:"Base-16 number system using digits 0-9 and letters A-F",example:"Colour code #FF5733 represents a shade of red-orange"},{term:"Binary Addition",definition:"Adding two binary numbers together, carrying over when a column totals 2 or more",example:"1011 + 0111 = 10010 (11 + 7 = 18 in denary)"},{term:"Overflow",definition:"An error that occurs when the result of a binary calculation needs more bits than are available; the extra bit is lost so the stored answer is incorrect",example:"Adding 11111111 + 00000001 in 8 bits gives 100000000 - the leading 1 is dropped and the result is recorded as 00000000"},{term:"Binary Shift",definition:"Moving all bits left or right; a left shift multiplies by 2, a right shift divides by 2",example:"00001100 shifted left by 1 becomes 00011000 (12 becomes 24)"},{term:"Most Significant Bit (MSB)",definition:"The bit with the highest place value in a binary number, usually the leftmost bit",example:"In 10110001, the MSB is 1 and represents 128"},{term:"Least Significant Bit (LSB)",definition:"The bit with the lowest place value in a binary number, usually the rightmost bit",example:"In 10110001, the LSB is 1 and represents 1"},{term:"Character Set",definition:"A defined collection of characters a computer can recognise, with each character mapped to a unique binary code; the size of the set determines how many characters can be represented",example:"ASCII and Unicode are character sets; using more bits per character allows more characters to be stored"},{term:"American Standard Code for Information Interchange (ASCII)",definition:"A 7-bit character set (8 bits in extended ASCII) representing 128 English characters: letters, digits, punctuation and control codes",example:"'A' is 65 (01000001), 'B' is 66, 'C' is 67; codes are logically ordered so 'B' is one more than 'A'"},{term:"Unicode",definition:"A character set that uses more bits per character than ASCII, so it can represent non-english characters plus symbols and emojis; its first 128 codes match ASCII",example:'A .txt file saved using Unicode can contain "你好 Hello ابحرم 🙂" all on one line; the same file saved as ASCII could only keep the "Hello" part'},{term:"Sound Sampling",definition:"Converting analogue sound into digital by measuring the amplitude at regular intervals",example:"Higher sample rate and bit depth produce more accurate sound but a larger file"},{term:"Sample Rate",definition:"The number of audio samples taken per second, measured in Hertz (Hz)",example:"CD quality audio uses 44,100 samples per second (44.1 kHz)"},{term:"Bit Depth",definition:"The number of bits used to record each audio sample; more bits means more detail",example:"16-bit audio gives 65,536 possible amplitude levels per sample"},{term:"Pixel",definition:"The smallest individual element of a digital image; each pixel stores a single colour value as a binary code",example:"A 1920 x 1080 image is made up of 2,073,600 pixels arranged in a grid"},{term:"Bitmap Image",definition:"A graphic made up of a grid of pixels, each assigned a colour value",example:"A 1920 x 1080 image contains over 2 million pixels"},{term:"Colour Depth",definition:"The number of bits used to represent the colour of each pixel",example:"24-bit colour depth gives approximately 16.7 million possible colours"},{term:"Resolution",definition:"The number of pixels in an image, usually width x height",example:"1920 x 1080 (Full High Definition)"},{term:"Metadata",definition:"Additional data stored alongside an image or sound file describing its properties so the file can be interpreted correctly",example:"An image file's metadata includes width, height, colour depth, date taken and camera model"},{term:"Image File Size Calculation",definition:"File size = width x height x colour depth (in bits), then convert to bytes",example:"100 x 100 pixels at 24-bit colour = 240,000 bits = 30,000 bytes"},{term:"Sound File Size Calculation",definition:"File size = sample rate x bit depth x duration (seconds), then convert to bytes",example:"44,100 x 16 x 10 seconds = 7,056,000 bits = 882,000 bytes"},{term:"Text File Size Calculation",definition:"File size = bits per character x number of characters, then convert bits to bytes if required",example:"A 1,000-character message in 8-bit ASCII = 1,000 x 8 = 8,000 bits = 1,000 bytes (1 KB)"},{term:"Compression",definition:"Reducing the size of a file so it takes up less storage and transfers more quickly across a network; can be lossy 