ver holds shared folders that students access from their PCs"},{term:"Peer-to-Peer (P2P) Network",definition:"All devices are equal and share resources directly with each other without a central server",example:"Two laptops sharing files directly over a home network"},{term:"Wired Connection (Ethernet)",definition:"Connecting devices using physical cables",example:"Category 6 (CAT6) cable plugged into a computer's network port"},{term:"Wireless Connection (Wi-Fi)",definition:"Connecting devices using radio waves transmitted by a Wireless Access Point (WAP)",example:"A phone connecting to a home router without cables"},{term:"Bluetooth",definition:"Short-range wireless technology for pairing devices over a few metres",example:"Wireless headphones connecting to a smartphone"},{term:"Internet Protocol (IP) Address",definition:"A unique numerical address that identifies a device on a network",example:"192.168.1.1 is a common address for a home router"},{term:"Media Access Control (MAC) Address",definition:"A unique hardware address permanently burned into a device's Network Interface Card (NIC)",example:"00:1A:2B:3C:4D:5E; set during manufacturing and cannot normally be changed"},{term:"TCP (Transmission Control Protocol)",definition:"A network protocol that checks packets are delivered reliably and reassembles them in the correct order",example:"When a web page is downloaded, TCP can request missing packets again so the page data arrives complete"},{term:"IP (Internet Protocol)",definition:"A network protocol that adds source and destination addresses so packets can be routed between networks",example:"Each packet travelling across the internet includes an IP address for the device it is being sent to"},{term:"HyperText Transfer Protocol (HTTP) / Secure (HTTPS)",definition:"Protocols for requesting and transferring web pages; HTTPS adds encryption via Transport Layer Security (TLS)",example:"Visiting https://www.bbc.co.uk; the padlock icon shows HTTPS is active"},{term:"File Transfer Protocol (FTP)",definition:"A protocol used for transferring files between a client and a server",example:"Uploading website files from a local computer to a web hosting server"},{term:"SMTP (Simple Mail Transfer Protocol)",definition:"The protocol used to send emails from a client to a server, or between mail servers",example:"When you click Send in Gmail, SMTP transfers your email to the recipient's mail server"},{term:"POP (Post Office Protocol)",definition:"An email protocol that downloads messages from the server to the local device and then removes them from the server",example:"Downloading emails to a desktop client using POP means they are no longer accessible from your phone"},{term:"IMAP (Internet Message Access Protocol)",definition:"An email protocol that keeps messages stored on the server and syncs them across all devices",example:"Reading an email on your phone and it also appearing as read on your laptop uses IMAP"},{term:"Email Protocols",definition:"A set of rules that govern how emails are sent, received, and stored across networks; SMTP handles sending, while POP and IMAP handle receiving",example:"When you send an email, SMTP delivers it to the recipient's server; when they check their inbox, POP or IMAP retrieves it"},{term:"Layering",definition:"The concept of dividing network communication into separate, independent layers where each layer has a specific role and communicates only with the layers directly above and below it",example:"Switching from Ethernet to Wi-Fi only changes the Network Interface Layer; the Application, Transport, and Internet layers above are unaffected"},{term:"Network Interface Card (NIC)",definition:"Hardware component that allows a device to connect to a network, either wired or wirelessly",example:"Every laptop has a built-in wireless NIC"},{term:"Router",definition:"A device that forwards packets between different networks and determines the best route",example:"Your home router sends data between your LAN and the internet (WAN)"},{term:"Switch",definition:"A device that connects devices within a LAN and sends data only to the intended recipient using MAC addresses",example:"A 24-port switch in a school server room connecting all classroom PCs"},{term:"Wireless Access Point (WAP)",definition:"A device that allows wireless devices to connect to a wired network",example:"A WAP mounted on a classroom ceiling providing Wi-Fi"},{term:"Transmission Media",definition:"The physical pathways that carry data signals between devices on a network, either wired (cables) or wireless (radio waves)",example:"Copper Ethernet cable, fibre optic cable and Wi-Fi radio waves are all transmission media"},{term:"Domain Name System (DNS)",definition:"A system of Domain Name Servers that converts human-readable domain names into IP addresses so browsers can locate websites",example:"Typing www.bbc.co.uk into a browser; the DNS converts this to an IP address like 151.101.0.81 so the request reaches the correct server"},{term:"The Cloud",definition:"Remote provision of services such as storage, software and processing power over the internet, accessed on demand rather than locally",example:"Google Drive stores files on remote servers; users access them from any device with an internet connection"},{term:"Hosting",definition:"The provision of server space and resources by a hosting company so that websites, applications or files are continuously available over the internet",example:"A web hosting company stores a school's website files on its servers so they can be reached at any time via the school's domain name"},{term:"Web Server and Client",definition:"A web server stores and delivers web pages; a client is a device or program that requests and displays those pages",example:"A web server hosts the BBC website; your browser acts as the client requesting and displaying the page"},{term:"Standards",definition:"Agreed rules and specifications that allow hardware and software from different manufacturers to work together",example:"Standards ensure a keyboard made by one company works with a computer made by another; they enable interoperability across the industry"}],"1.4":[{term:"Malware",definition:"Malicious software designed to damage systems or gain unauthorised access",example:"Virus, trojan, spyware, ransomware, worm"},{term:"Virus",definition:"Malware that attaches to a legitimate file and spreads when the file is executed",example:"A virus hidden inside a downloaded game installer that corrupts files when run"},{term:"Trojan",definition:"Malware disguised as legitimate software that creates a backdoor for attackers",example:"A free app that secretly installs a keylogger to record passwords"},{term:"Spyware",definition:"Malware that secretly monitors user activity and sends data to a third party",example:"Software tracking every website you visit and sending it to advertisers"},{term:"Ransomware",definition:"Malware that encrypts a victim's files and demands payment for the decryption key",example:"WannaCry attack in 2017 locked hospital computers and demanded Bitcoin"},{term:"Worm",definition:"Self-replicating malware that spreads automatically across networks without needing a host file or user action",example:"A worm exploits a vulnerability in connected computers and copies itself between them, slowing the network as it spreads"},{term:"Phishing",definition:"Fake communication (usually email) designed to trick users into revealing personal data",example:"An email pretending to be from a bank asking you to click a link and enter your login details"},{term:"Brute Force Attack",definition:"Trying every possible password combination systematically until the correct one is found",example:"Software cycling through millions of password combinations per second"},{term:"Denial of Service (DoS) Attack",definition:"Flooding a server with so many requests that it becomes overloaded and unavailable to legitimate users",example:"Overloading an online 