wn":[{"criteria":"Normal: Any normal value (between 40 and 180 inclusive)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Boundary: 40.0 // 180.0","marks":1},{"criteria":"Erroneous: Any value less than 40 // any value greater than 180 // any non-numeric value","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(d)","parent_question":5,"topic":"sql-where-clause","marks":4,"command_word":"Complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The individual results for each student in each event are stored in a database.\\n\\nThe database table TblResult stores the times of students in the 100 m race. Some of the data is shown."},{"type":"table","headers":["StudentID","YearGroup","TeamName","Time"],"rows":[["11GC1","11","Valiants","20.3"],["10VE1","10","Super-Team","19.7"],["10SM1","10","Super-Team","19.2"],["11JP2","11","Champions","19.65"]]},{"type":"text","value":"Complete the SQL statement to show the Student ID and team name of all students who are in year group 11."},{"type":"code","language":"sql","value":"SELECT StudentID, ____\\nFROM ____\\n________________"}],"answer":{"guidance":"Max 3 if not in correct order / includes other logical errors. Ignore capitals. Do not accept * or additional fields for BP1.\\n\\nSpelling must be accurate (e.g. not TblResults). No spaces in field names, penalise obvious spaces once and then FT. Allow quotation marks around field names, table name and 11. Accept == for BP4 (invalid SQL but works in some environments).","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"TeamName only in first space","marks":1},{"criteria":"TblResult in second space","marks":1},{"criteria":"WHERE","marks":1},{"criteria":"YearGroup = 11","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(e)(i)","parent_question":5,"topic":"abstraction-in-design","marks":1,"command_word":"Identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Abstraction and decomposition have been used in the design of the sports day program.\\n\\nIdentify one way that abstraction has been used in the design of this program."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Any example of simplification / removing data or focussing on data (in the design) that is applicable to this program (in the context of students and a sports day), not a generic description of what abstraction is.\\n\\nExamples: \\"focus on student names and events\\"; \\"ignore data such as students\' favourite subjects\\"; \\"store year groups instead of ages or DOB\\"; \\"shows student IDs instead of full student details\\".","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Any example of simplification / removing data or focussing on data in context of sports day","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(e)(ii)","parent_question":5,"topic":"decomposition-in-design","marks":1,"command_word":"Identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Identify one way that decomposition has been used in the design of this program."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Any example of breaking down the program into sections/subroutines, or breaking down the database into tables. Must be applicable to this program, not a generic description.\\n\\nExamples: \\"splits the program up into different events\\"; \\"separates the validation routines into subroutines\\"; \\"breaks the database down into a table per event\\". Do not give answers discussing splitting into fields. Allow reference to sports day to mean sports day program.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Any example of breaking the program into sections/subroutines OR database into tables (in context)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(f)","parent_question":5,"topic":"highest-score-iteration","marks":6,"command_word":"Write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"An algorithm works out which team has won (has the highest score).\\n\\nWrite an algorithm to:\\n- prompt the user to enter a team name and score, or to enter \\"stop\\" to stop entering new teams\\n- repeatedly take team names and scores as input until the user enters \\"stop\\"\\n- calculate which team has the highest score\\n- output the team name and score of the winning team in an appropriate message.\\n\\nYou must use either:\\n- OCR Exam Reference Language, or\\n- A high-level programming language that you have studied."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"Example 1:"},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"highscore = 0\\nwhile team != \\"stop\\"\\n  team = input(\\"enter team name\\")\\n  score = input(\\"enter score\\")\\n  if score > highscore then\\n    highscore = score\\n    highteam = team\\n  endif\\nendwhile\\nprint(highscore)\\nprint(highteam)"},{"type":"list","items":["For BP3, allow \\"stop\\" for either team name or score (or both).","Allow use of break to exit loop.","Allow recursive function(s) for BP2 and BP3.","Initialisation of variables is not needed.","Ignore that multiple teams could get the same high score; assume only one team has the highest score.","BP4 and BP5 could be done in many ways. Allow use of max/sum functions and use of arrays/lists.","FT for BP6 if an attempt is made at calculating highest score/name.","If an answer simply asks for multiple entries without iteration, BP2 and BP3 cannot be accessed, but all other marks remain available.","`input` cannot be used as a variable name."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Input team name AND score and store/use separately","marks":1},{"criteria":"Attempt at using iteration","marks":1},{"criteria":"...to enter team/score until \\"stop\\" entered","marks":1},{"criteria":"Calculates highest score","marks":1},{"criteria":"Calculates winning team name","marks":1},{"criteria":"Outputs highest score and team name","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"6","parent_question":6,"topic":"flowchart-odd-even","marks":4,"command_word":"Complete","response_style":"annotate-image","content":[{"type":"text","value":"An algorithm decides if a number is odd or even.\\n\\nAn odd number divided by 2 will give the remainder 1.\\n\\nThe flowchart statements have been written for the algorithm, but the flowchart is incomplete.\\n\\nComplete the flowchart."},{"type":"image","kind":"diagram","src":"j277/topics/ocr/2.1.2_q6_odd_even_flowc