' into the correct place without discussing how this is determined. Max one if generic answer with no reference to given array. Allow answers referring to swapping items down the array to get to the correct position.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Compare 'or' with next item on left","marks":1},{"criteria":"'or' is smaller than 'when' so it repeats the comparison with next item and inserts 'or' / item in correct place, based on comparisons","marks":1},{"criteria":"Repeat to insert 'it'","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"4d","parent_question":4,"topic":"merge sort identification","marks":1,"command_word":"state","content":[{"type":"text","value":"One sorting algorithm splits an array into single items and then combines them into an ordered array. State the name of this sorting algorithm."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Allow other sorting algorithms that use divide and conquer.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Merge sort","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5a","parent_question":5,"topic":"purpose of temp variable in insertion sort","marks":2,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"An insertion sort is one type of sorting algorithm. A student has written a pseudocode algorithm to perform an insertion sort on a 1D array names."},{"type":"code","language":"erl","value":"names = [\\"Kareem\\", \\"Sarah\\", \\"Zac\\", \\"Sundip\\", \\"Anika\\"]\\nfor count = 1 to names.length - 1\\n  pos = count\\n  while (pos > 0 and names[pos] < names[pos - 1])\\n    temp = names[pos]\\n    names[pos] = names[pos - 1]\\n    names[pos - 1] = temp\\n    pos = pos - 1\\n  endwhile\\nnext count"},{"type":"text","value":"Describe the purpose of the variable temp in the insertion sort pseudocode algorithm."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Do not allow answers that clearly refer to storing the position / index (or any other out-of-context data) for BP1; it is the name itself that is being stored, not the position. If unclear, allow BOD. E.g. do not allow 'holds the values of the index / holds value for position of the name'. Allow FT for subsequent points.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Stores/holds data/value/name/names[pos]","marks":1},{"criteria":"...so (value) can be changed / swapped / moved / overwritten / inserted / without being lost / will be assigned to names[pos-1]","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5b","parent_question":5,"topic":"condition-controlled inner loop","marks":2,"command_word":"explain","content":[{"type":"text","value":"An insertion sort contains a nested loop; a loop within a loop. In this pseudocode algorithm the outer loop is a count-controlled loop and the inner loop is a condition-controlled loop."},{"type":"text","value":"Explain why the inner loop needs to be a condition-controlled loop."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Max 1 from each section, 2 marks total. Do not allow 'while names are in the wrong order'. BP4 must have reference to checking a condition / condition being met, not just having a condition.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Reason (1 mark): do not know how many iterations / swaps needed, or do not know (at run time) how many times the value will change positions, or do not know how many times a condition-controlled loop will need to run / execute","marks":1},{"criteria":"Description (1 mark): condition-controlled loops run while/until a condition is true/false/met; repeats while value in [pos-1] is larger than value in [pos] / while further swap needed; will swap value until in correct position; more efficient than / does not need to iterate as many times as a count-controlled / for loop","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5ci","parent_question":5,"topic":"difference between insertion sort and bubble sort","marks":2,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A bubble sort is a type of sorting algorithm."},{"type":"text","value":"Describe one difference between an insertion sort and a bubble sort."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark each for insertion and bubble sort, max 2. Answer must reference both bubble sort and insertion sort for 2 marks (except if efficiency mark plus expansion given). Allow reference to Big O for efficiency discussion. Only award efficiency once. Only award fewer iterations once. Do not accept 'completes in one iteration' for insertion sort. Accept list / data / values / etc for array. 'When data more scrambled' only makes sense when discussing efficiency/speed; do not give marks for saying that either can handle data that is more scrambled. Do not accept 'bubble/insertion sort does not' for 2nd mark.\\n\\nInsertion sort points: inserts/moves values into correct position; inserts value once (then in correct position); stops when end of array reached / completes in one pass through the array; moves items down the array / left; start of array becomes sorted first; creates a sorted array within an array / has a sorted/unsorted partition; starts on 2nd value; more efficient/faster than bubble sort; ...because fewer iterations/comparisons on average; ...when data more scrambled.\\n\\nBubble sort points: compares/swaps pairs of values; value is repeatedly moved/swapped (until in correct position); repeats if a swap has been made / needs multiple passes; will complete a final iteration once sorted (to check for no swaps needed); moves items up the array; end of array becomes sorted first; moves/bubbles the highest value to the top; less efficient/slower than insertion sort (on large sets of values); ...more iterations/comparisons on average; ...when data more scrambled.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"One valid point describing insertion sort behaviour","marks":1},{"criteria":"One valid point describing bubble sort behaviour (contrasting)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5cii","parent_question":5,"topic":"similarities between insertion sort and bubble sort","marks":2,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Describe two similarities between an insertion sort and a bubble sort."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark each to max 2. Allow refere