 sort","marks":1},{"criteria":"Row 3 (check each item one at a time) identified as Linear search","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"4(c)","parent_question":4,"topic":"binary search prerequisite","marks":1,"command_word":"state","content":[{"type":"text","value":"State why a binary search cannot be used on the list of numbers in part (a) without first carrying out another step."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"1 mark."},{"type":"list","items":["The list is not sorted / the list is in a random order / binary search requires a sorted list","Accept \'the list must be sorted first\' or any equivalent statement that the data must be in order."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Identifies that binary search requires the list to be sorted (or that the list is unsorted)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(a)","parent_question":5,"topic":"low-level language reasons","marks":1,"command_word":"give","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Give one reason why a programmer might choose to write a program in a **low-level** language rather than a high-level language."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"1 mark for any one of the following points (or any other valid reason):"},{"type":"list","items":["Allows direct control of / direct access to the hardware","Programs run more quickly / are more efficient","Programs use less memory","Useful when memory is limited (e.g. embedded systems, microcontrollers)","Allows the use of specific processor instructions"]},{"type":"text","value":"Do not accept \'easier to write\' or \'easier to debug\' (these are advantages of high-level languages, not low-level)."}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"One valid reason for choosing a low-level language given","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"5(b)","parent_question":5,"topic":"compiler vs interpreter","marks":4,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Describe **two** differences between a compiler and an interpreter."},{"type":"text","value":"Difference 1: ____"},{"type":"text","value":"Difference 2: ____"}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"1 mark for each correct point about a compiler, 1 mark for each correct contrasting point about an interpreter (max 4). Each difference must be a clear comparison — a description of one translator alone is not enough."},{"type":"text","value":"Valid difference points include:"},{"type":"list","items":["A compiler translates the **whole** program at once / before running, whereas an interpreter translates the program **line by line** as it runs","A compiler produces an **executable file** (which can be re-used) whereas an interpreter does not / re-translates each time the program is run","A compiled program runs **faster** than an interpreted program (once compiled)","A compiler reports **all errors at the end** of compilation, whereas an interpreter **stops at the first error** found","A compiled program does not need the translator to be present to run, whereas an interpreted program does"]},{"type":"text","value":"Allow descriptions in either direction (compiler-first or interpreter-first). Do not accept \'a compiler is faster\' without explaining why or what it is faster than."}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"First difference — point about the compiler","marks":1},{"criteria":"First difference — contrasting point about the interpreter","marks":1},{"criteria":"Second difference — point about the compiler","marks":1},{"criteria":"Second difference — contrasting point about the interpreter","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"6(a)","parent_question":6,"topic":"syntax error definition","marks":1,"command_word":"state","content":[{"type":"text","value":"State what is meant by a **syntax error** in a program."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"1 mark."},{"type":"list","items":["An error in the rules / grammar of the programming language","Accept any equivalent: \'breaks the rules of the language\', \'code does not follow the syntax of the language\', \'prevents the program from running / being translated\'","Do not accept generic statements like \'the program does not work\' — the answer must reference rules / grammar / syntax of the language."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Definition references the rules / grammar / syntax of the programming language being broken","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"6(b)","parent_question":6,"topic":"logic error identification","marks":3,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"An algorithm is meant to take 5 marks as input, calculate the average, and output `\\"Pass\\"` if the average is 50 or above, otherwise output `\\"Fail\\"`."},{"type":"text","value":"The algorithm contains three logic errors."},{"type":"code","language":"ocr-reference","value":"01 total = 0\\n02 for i = 1 to 5\\n03   mark = input(\\"Enter mark\\")\\n04   total = mark\\n05 next i\\n06 average = total * 5\\n07 if average > 50 then\\n08   print(\\"Pass\\")\\n09 else\\n10   print(\\"Fail\\")\\n11 endif"},{"type":"text","value":"Identify the line number and describe the logic error on that line, for each of the three errors."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"1 mark for each of the three logic errors correctly identified with line number and brief description."},{"type":"list","items":["**Line 04** — Should accumulate (`total = total + mark`) rather than overwrite the total each iteration","**Line 06** — Should divide by 5 (`total / 5`), not multiply, to calculate the average","**Line 07** — Should be `>= 50` (greater than or equal to) so that exactly 50 is a pass, not strictly greater than 50"]},{"type":"text","value":"Each error must include both the line number and the nature of the mistake. Accept \'add the mark to total\' / \'should be addition\' for line 04. Accept \'divide instead of multiply\' for line 06. Accept \'should include 50\' / \'use >=\' for line 07. Do not award the mark if a correct line is identified but the description is wrong (or vice versa)."}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteri