s":1}]}},{"question_id":"7(b)(i)","parent_question":7,"topic":"maintainability improvements","marks":4,"command_word":"describe","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A developer is asked to update the GreenRide program written by another programmer. The original code is hard to maintain."},{"type":"text","value":"Describe **two** ways the original programmer could have written the code to make it easier to maintain."},{"type":"text","value":"Method 1: ____"},{"type":"text","value":"Description: ____"},{"type":"text","value":"Method 2: ____"},{"type":"text","value":"Description: ____"}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"1 mark for each method named, 1 mark for each matching description (max 4). Description must add detail beyond simply naming the method."},{"type":"text","value":"Valid methods and matching descriptions include:"},{"type":"list","items":["**Comments** — explain what each section / function of the code does, so a new developer can understand its purpose without tracing it","**Meaningful identifier names** — using descriptive names such as `bikeID` rather than `b1`, so the purpose of each variable is clear","**Indentation** — consistently indenting code inside loops and selection blocks so the structure of the program is visible","**Sub-programs / functions / procedures** — breaking the code into reusable named blocks, so changes only need to be made in one place","**Constants** — using a named constant (e.g. `RATE_PER_MIN`) for fixed values so they can be updated in one place","**Whitespace / blank lines** — separating logical sections of code so they are easier to read"]},{"type":"text","value":"Do not accept the same method named twice with different wording (e.g. \'comments\' and \'annotations\'). Do not credit defensive design / validation as a maintainability technique. Description must explain *how* the technique helps maintainability — naming the technique alone is not enough for the second mark."}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Method 1 — valid maintainability technique named","marks":1},{"criteria":"Method 1 — matching description that explains how it improves maintainability","marks":1},{"criteria":"Method 2 — different valid maintainability technique named","marks":1},{"criteria":"Method 2 — matching description that explains how it improves maintainability","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"7(b)(ii)","parent_question":7,"topic":"IDE features identification","marks":2,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Identify **two** features of an IDE (other than the editor itself) that could help the developer when testing the GreenRide program."},{"type":"text","value":"Feature 1: ____"},{"type":"text","value":"Feature 2: ____"}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"1 mark per valid IDE feature named (max 2). Two distinct features required."},{"type":"list","items":["Translator / compiler / interpreter","Run-time environment / output window","Error reporting / diagnostics","Debugger","Stepping / step-through","Variable watch","Break points","Pretty printing / keyword (syntax) highlighting","Keyword / auto-completion"]},{"type":"text","value":"Do not accept \'editor\' or \'IDE\' as one of the answers. Do not accept the same feature twice (e.g. \'compiler\' and \'interpreter\' count as one feature). Ignore minor spelling errors."}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"First valid IDE feature named (other than the editor)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Second distinct valid IDE feature named","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"7(c)(i)","parent_question":7,"topic":"validation algorithm completion","marks":4,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Each bike\'s `bikeID` must be the letter `\\"B\\"` followed by exactly **3 digits** (for example, `\\"B042\\"`)."},{"type":"text","value":"An algorithm is being written to validate a `bikeID` entered by the user. If the `bikeID` is valid, `\\"valid\\"` is output, otherwise `\\"invalid\\"` is output."},{"type":"text","value":"Complete the algorithm."},{"type":"code","language":"ocr-reference","value":"bikeID = input(\\"Enter the bike ID\\")\\nif length(bikeID) ____ 4 AND ____ then\\n  print(\\"____\\")\\nelse\\n  print(____)\\nendif"}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"1 mark per gap to max 4."},{"type":"list","items":["Gap 1: `==` (or `=` in OCR Reference Language)","Gap 2: A valid check that the first character of `bikeID` is `\\"B\\"` (e.g. `bikeID[0] == \\"B\\"` or `bikeID.subString(0,1) == \\"B\\"`)","Gap 3: `valid`","Gap 4: `\\"invalid\\"` (must be enclosed in quotation marks)"]},{"type":"code","language":"ocr-reference","value":"bikeID = input(\\"Enter the bike ID\\")\\nif length(bikeID) == 4 AND bikeID[0] == \\"B\\" then\\n  print(\\"valid\\")\\nelse\\n  print(\\"invalid\\")\\nendif"},{"type":"list","items":["Accept any logically equivalent first-character check (e.g. `left(bikeID, 1) == \\"B\\"`, `bikeID.startsWith(\\"B\\")`).","Do not accept `<= 4` or `>= 4` for Gap 1 — exact length is required.","Ignore minor spelling and capitalisation errors in Gaps 3 and 4 as long as quotation marks are present.","Do not award Gap 2 if the candidate writes `bikeID == \\"B\\"` (this checks the entire string)."]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Gap 1: == (exact equality with 4)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Gap 2: valid check that the first character is \\"B\\"","marks":1},{"criteria":"Gap 3: valid (in quotation marks)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Gap 4: \\"invalid\\" (in quotation marks)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"7(c)(ii)","parent_question":7,"topic":"test plan completion","marks":3,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Complete the test plan for the validation algorithm in part (i) by adding **one** example of test data and the expected outcome for each test type."},{"type":"table","headers":["Test type","Test data","Expected outcome"],"rows":[["Normal","",""],["Boundary","",""],["Invalid / erroneous","",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"1 mark per row, awarded only when *both* the test data and the expected outcome are sensible for that test type."},{"type":"list","items":["**Normal** — any valid `bikeID` of `\\"B\\"` plus 3 digits, but **not** at the boundary (e.g. `\\"B042\\"`, `\\"B500\\"`) → expected outcome `\\"valid\\"`","**Boundary** — exactly 4 characters of the form `\\"B\\"` + 3 digits, **or** input that is exactly 1 longer / shorter than the allowed length to test the limit (e.g. `\\"B000\\"`, `\\"B999\\"`, or for failing-boundary `\\"B12\\"` / `\\"B0001\\"`) → expected outcome correctly stated for the test data given","**Invalid / erroneous** — input that clearly breaks the rule (e.g. `\\"X042\\"`, `\\"042\\"`, `\\"\\"`, `\\"B12345\\"`) → expected outcome `\\"invalid\\"`"]},{"type":"text","value":"Allow follow-through of expected outcome based on the test data given. Do not award the mark if either the test data or the expected outcome is missing or wrong. Accept any sensible bike ID format for the test data."}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Normal test data with matching expected outcome \\"valid\\"","marks":1},{"criteria":"Boundary test data with matching expected outcome","marks":1},{"criteria":"Invalid / erroneous test data with matching expected outcome \\"invalid\\"","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"7(d)","parent_question":7,"topic":"SQL query rides","marks":4,"command_word":"write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"GreenRide stores ride data in a database table called `tblRides`. The table fields are shown below:"},{"type":"table","headers":["Field","Description"],"rows":[["`RideID`","The unique identifier of the ride"],["`MemberID`","The member who took the ride"],["`BikeID`","The bike used for the ride"],["`Minutes`","The length of the ride, in whole minutes"],["`DistanceKm`","The distance of the ride, in kilometres"]]},{"type":"text","value":"Some of the data in `tblRides` is shown:"},{"type":"table","headers":["`RideID`","`MemberID`","`BikeID`","`Minutes`","`DistanceKm`"],"rows":[