, or\\n• a high-level programming language that you have studied."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"1 mark per bullet to max 4. Section B, so the answer must be high-level code (not pseudocode in plain English). Award the mark for each of:"},{"type":"list","items":["Function definition for `calculateFare` with one parameter (the parameter must not be overwritten — no `input()` inside the function)","Calculates the fare by multiplying the parameter by 0.2","Selection (if / else) that compares the calculated fare against 15","Returns the correct value (15 if over the cap, otherwise the calculated fare). Must use `return`, not `print`."]},{"type":"code","language":"ocr-reference","value":"function calculateFare(minutes)\\n  fare = minutes * 0.2\\n  if fare > 15 then\\n    return 15\\n  else\\n    return fare\\n  endif\\nendfunction"},{"type":"list","items":["Accept equivalent: `return min(fare, 15)` if the language supports it","Accept `>= 15` returning 15 (slightly different but acceptable)","Accept `15.0` or `15.00` for the cap value","Do not accept `x` for multiplication","Do not accept spaces in the function or parameter name","Accept assigning to the function name (e.g. `calculateFare = fare`) for languages such as VB"]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Function definition for `calculateFare` with one parameter (not overwritten)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Calculates fare by multiplying the parameter by 0.2","marks":1},{"criteria":"Selection comparing the calculated fare against the £15 cap","marks":1},{"criteria":"Returns 15 when over the cap and the calculated fare otherwise (uses return, not print)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"7(e)(ii)","parent_question":7,"topic":"function call usage","marks":2,"command_word":"write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Write an algorithm that:"},{"type":"text","value":"• uses the function `calculateFare()` to find the fare for a ride that lasted 25 minutes\\n• stores the result in the variable `totalCost`."},{"type":"text","value":"You must use either:"},{"type":"text","value":"• OCR Exam Reference Language, or\\n• a high-level programming language that you have studied."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"list","items":["1 mark — calls `calculateFare` with `25` as the argument","1 mark — assigns the returned value to `totalCost`"]},{"type":"code","language":"ocr-reference","value":"totalCost = calculateFare(25)"},{"type":"list","items":["Do not accept `==` for assignment","Do not accept definitions of the function — the answer must call the existing function","Allow multiple values passed in as long as `25` is one of them and matches the function definition"]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"`calculateFare` called with `25` as the argument","marks":1},{"criteria":"Returned value assigned to `totalCost` (e.g. `totalCost = calculateFare(25)`)","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"7(f)","parent_question":7,"topic":"2D array element access","marks":3,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"GreenRide stores the contents of `tblRides` from part (d) in a 2D array called `arrRides`. Each row stores one ride. The columns are stored in this order: `RideID`, `MemberID`, `BikeID`, `Minutes`, `DistanceKm`."},{"type":"text","value":"The first ride is stored in row 0 of the array, the second ride in row 1, and so on."},{"type":"text","value":"Complete the table to show the value stored at each location of the array."},{"type":"table","headers":["Array reference","Value stored"],"rows":[["`arrRides[0,2]`",""],["`arrRides[3,3]`",""],["`arrRides[4,1]`",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"1 mark per correct value. Using the row, column convention as set up in the question:"},{"type":"list","items":["`arrRides[0,2]` — row 0 (Ride 1), column 2 (`BikeID`) → `\\"B042\\"`","`arrRides[3,3]` — row 3 (Ride 4), column 3 (`Minutes`) → `55`","`arrRides[4,1]` — row 4 (Ride 5), column 1 (`MemberID`) → `\\"M002\\"`"]},{"type":"text","value":"Accept values without quotation marks. Ignore minor formatting differences (e.g. `B042` vs `\\"B042\\"`). If the candidate has used column-major instead of row-major (i.e. swapped indices), allow follow-through provided the convention is consistent across all three answers."}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"`arrRides[0,2]` = `\\"B042\\"`","marks":1},{"criteria":"`arrRides[3,3]` = `55`","marks":1},{"criteria":"`arrRides[4,1]` = `\\"M002\\"`","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"7(g)","parent_question":7,"topic":"bike booking algorithm","marks":6,"command_word":"write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"There are 50 bikes available at the GreenRide docking station at the start of the day. The number of bikes currently available is stored in the variable `bikesAvailable`."},{"type":"text","value":"Write an algorithm that:"},{"type":"text","value":"• prompts the user to enter how many bikes they want to book and takes this value as input\\n• outputs `\\"Booking confirmed\\"` and reduces the value of `bikesAvailable` if there are enough bikes\\n• outputs `\\"Not enough bikes available\\"` if there are not enough bikes\\n• repeats until there are no bikes left."},{"type":"text","value":"You must use either:"},{"type":"text","value":"• OCR Exam Reference Language, or\\n• a high-level programming language that you have studied."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"1 mark per bullet to max 6. Award the mark for each of:"},{"type":"list","items":["Input and store / use the number of bikes the user wants","Check if there are enough bikes left (must allow the exact number — e.g. 5 needed and 5 remaining, so use `>=` not `>`)","Sensible confirmation messages output correctly for both the \'booked\' and \'not enough\' cases","`bikesAvailable` updated correctly (subtract only when booking succeeds)","Attempt at iteration (loop construct present)","Repeats all of the above while `bikesAvailable > 0` (or equivalent — `until bikesAvailable <= 0`), without resetting `bikesAvailable` to 50"]},{"type":"code","language":"ocr-reference","value":"while bikesAvailable > 0\\n  num = input(\\"How many bikes?\\")\\n  if bikesAvailable >= num then\\n    print(\\"Booking confirmed\\")\\n    bikesAvailable = bikesAvailable - num\\n  else\\n    print(\\"Not enough bikes available\\")\\n  endif\\nendwhile"},{"type":"text","value":"BP6 must not repeatedly reset `bikesAvailable` to 50 and must include the repeated input. Allow follow-through if a previous BP is missing or incorrect, where logically possible."}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Input and store / use the number of bikes the user wants","marks":1},{"criteria":"Check if there are enough bikes left (allows exact match, e.g. `bikesAvailable >= num`)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Sensible confirmation messages output correctly for both the \'booked\' and \'not enough\' cases","marks":1},{"criteria":"`bikesAvailable` updated correctly (subtracted only when booking succeeds)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Attempt at iteration (loop construct present)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Loop repeats the whole process while `bikesAvailable > 0` (or equivalent), without resetting `bikesAvailable`","marks":1}]}},{"question_id":"7(h)","parent_question":7,"topic":"longest ride algorithm","marks":6,"command_word":"write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"An algorithm is needed to find the longest ride taken by any member."},{"type":"text","value":"Write an algorithm to:"},{"type":"text","value":"• prompt the user to enter a member ID and the length of a ride in minutes, or to enter `\\"end\\"` to stop\\n• repeatedly take member IDs and ride lengths as input until the user enters `\\"end\\"`\\n• calculate which member had the longest single ride\\n• output the member ID and the length of that ride in an appropriate message."},{"type":"text","value":"You must use either:"},{"type":"text","value":"• OCR Exam Reference Language, or\\n• a high-level programming language that you have studied."}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"1 mark per bullet to max 6. Award the mark for each of:"},{"type":"list","items":["**BP1:** Inputs both `memberID` and ride length, and stores / uses them separately","**BP2:** Attempt at iteration (e.g. `while` loop)","**BP3:** Iteration continues until `\\"end\\"` is entered","**BP4:** Tracks the longest ride length seen so far (running max)","**BP5:** Tracks the corresponding member ID for the longest ride (updated when a new max is found)","**BP6:** Outputs the longest ride length and the matching member ID in an appropriate message"]},{"type":"text","value":"Example 1 (running max):"},{"type":"code","language":"ocr-reference","value":"longest = 0\\nwhile memberID != \\"end\\"\\n  memberID = input(\\"Enter member ID or end\\")\\n  if memberID != \\"end\\" then\\n    minutes = input(\\"Enter ride length\\")\\n    if minutes > longest then\\n      longest = minutes\\n      topMember = memberID\\n    endif\\n  endif\\nendwhile\\nprint(\\"Longest ride: \\" + longest + \\" minutes by \\" + topMember)"},{"type":"text","value":"Example 2 (array-based):"},{"type":"code","language":"ocr-reference","value":"members = []\\nrides = []\\nwhile memberID != \\"end\\"\\n  memberID = input(\\"Enter member ID or end\\")\\n  if memberID != \\"end\\" then\\n    minutes = input(\\"Enter ride length\\")\\n    members.append(memberID)\\n    rides.append(minutes)\\n  endif\\nendwhile\\nlongest = max(rides)\\ntopMember = members[rides.index(longest)]\\nprint(\\"Longest ride:\\", longest, \\"by\\", topMember)"},{"type":"list","items":["For BP3 allow `\\"end\\"` to be entered for either field as the trigger to stop","Allow `break` (or equivalent) to exit the loop for BP3","Initialisation of variables not needed — assume variables are 0 / empty if not set","Assume only one member has the longest ride (no tie-break needed)","Allow follow-through for BP6 if BP4 or BP5 was attempted","If the answer asks for multiple entries without using iteration, BP2 and BP3 cannot be accessed but BP1, BP4, BP5, BP6 are still available","For minor syntax errors (missing quotation marks, `==` for assignment, spaces in variable names), penalise once then follow-through","`input` cannot be used as a variable name"]}],"marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Input member ID AND ride length and store / use them separately","marks":1},{"criteria":"Attempt at using iteration (e.g. while loop)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Iteration continues until \\"end\\" is entered","marks":1},{"criteria":"Calculates / tracks the longest ride length","marks":1},{"criteria":"Tracks the corresponding member ID for the longest ride","marks":1},{"criteria":"Outputs the longest ride length and the matching member ID in an appropriate message","marks":1}]}}]'),tre={paper_id:Hae,qualification:Gae,component:Zae,title:Yae,year:Xae,session:Kae,total_marks:$ae,questions:ere},are="J277-02-2022-June",rre="J277",ire="02",sre="Computational Thinking Algorithms And Programming",nre=2022,ore="June",cre=80,lre=JSON.parse('[{"question_id":"1(a)","parent_question":1,"topic":"selection and iteration","marks":4,"command_word":"tick","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Tick one box in each row to identify whether the OCR Reference Language code given is an example of selection or iteration."},{"type":"table","headers":["OCR Reference Language code","Selection","Iteration"],"rows":[["for i = 1 to 10\\n  print(i)\\nnext i","",""],["while score != 0\\n  playgame()\\nendwhile","",""],["if playerHit() then\\n  score = 0\\nendif","",""],["switch bonus:\\n  case 0:\\n    score = 9\\n  case 1:\\n    score = 7\\n  case 2:\\n    score = 5\\nendswitch","",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":[{"type":"text","value":"1 mark per correct row. No mark is given if both boxes in a row are ticked. Accept any response (ticks, crosses, etc.) that clearly indicates the candidate\'s choice."},{"type":"list","items":["Row 1 (for i = 1 to 10) is Iteration","Row 2 (while score != 0) is Iteration","Row 3 (if playerHit() then) is Selection","Row 4 (switch bonus) is Selection"]}],"mark