utational_thinking_algorithms_and_programming_Practice_2019 (1).pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/question/page-006.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/markscheme/page-019.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/dev-qa/q-3-a.png"}},{"question_id":"3(b)","parent_question":3,"topic":"Boolean algorithm completion","marks":3,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Complete the following algorithm to output whether the customer is allowed to see the film or not."},{"type":"code","value":"A = input(\\"Is the customer 15 or over?\\")\\nB = input(\\"Does the customer have a ticket?\\")\\nC = input(\\"Does the customer have money to buy a ticket?\\")"}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark per bullet to max 3. Points: logically compares A AND (correct nested IF); B OR C (correct sequential IF); outputs in both cases (with an attempt at selection). Example: if A AND (B OR C) then print(\\"allowed\\") else print(\\"not allowed\\") endif. Accept answers where inputs are compared as strings (e.g. if A == \\"Yes\\" AND (B == \\"Yes\\" OR C == \\"Yes\\")).","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Correct logical comparison using AND with A (e.g. if A AND ...)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Correct logical comparison using OR between B and C (e.g. (B OR C))","marks":1},{"criteria":"Outputs one message if the condition is True and another if False (attempt at selection), e.g. print(\\"allowed\\") / print(\\"not allowed\\")","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/yearpapers/J27702_Computational_thinking_algorithms_and_programming_Practice_2019 (1).pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/question/page-007.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/markscheme/page-019.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/dev-qa/q-3-b.png"}},{"question_id":"3(c)","parent_question":3,"topic":"function call usage","marks":2,"command_word":"write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The cinema has three screens: \\"Red\\", \\"Black\\" and \\"Yellow\\"."},{"type":"text","value":"The function \`freeseats()\` counts how many seats are available in each screen. The name of the screen is passed in as a string parameter and the number of free seats is returned as an integer."},{"type":"text","value":"Write code using the function \`freeseats()\` to find the number of seats available in screen Red and assign this to a variable with identifier \`redseats\`."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for calling freeseats with \\"Red\\" as the argument; 1 mark for assigning the returned value to the variable redseats. Example: redseats = freeseats(\\"Red\\"). \\"Red\\" must use suitable string delimiters (e.g. speech marks) if the string is passed directly. Do not penalise case.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"freeseats called with \\"Red\\" as the argument (using appropriate string delimiters)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Returned value assigned to variable redseats (e.g. redseats = freeseats(\\"Red\\"))","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/yearpapers/J27702_Computational_thinking_algorithms_and_programming_Practice_2019 (1).pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/question/page-007.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/markscheme/page-019.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/dev-qa/q-3-c.png"}},{"question_id":"4(a)","parent_question":4,"topic":"car dealership scenario","marks":0,"command_word":"context","content":[{"type":"text","value":"A car dealership uses a computer system to record details of the cars that it has for sale. Each car has a make, model, age and number of miles driven."},{"type":"text","value":"Each car is given a star rating of 1 to 5, based on the age of the car and the number of miles it has been driven. This rating is recorded in the computer system."}],"answer":{"guidance":"Context for parts (i), (ii) and (iii). No marks.","marks_breakdown":[]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/yearpapers/J27702_Computational_thinking_algorithms_and_programming_Practice_2019 (1).pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/question/page-008.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/markscheme/page-020.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/dev-qa/q-4-a.png"}},{"question_id":"4(a)(i)","parent_question":4,"topic":"abstraction definition","marks":1,"command_word":"define","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Define the term abstraction."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for a definition that refers to hiding, ignoring or removing detail / focussing on certain parts of a problem.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Definition: hiding / ignoring / removing detail, or focussing on certain parts of a problem","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/yearpapers/J27702_Computational_thinking_algorithms_and_programming_Practice_2019 (1).pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/question/page-008.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/markscheme/page-020.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/dev-qa/q-4-a-i.png"}},{"question_id":"4(a)(ii)","parent_question":4,"topic":"abstraction example","marks":1,"command_word":"give","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Give one example of how abstraction has been used in the design of this star rating system."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark for a valid example of abstraction in this context: focus on the age and/or number of miles of the car, while ignoring other factors such as make or model.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Example referring to focusing on age / number of miles and ignoring other factors (e.g. make, model)","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/yearpapers/J27702_Computational_thinking_algorithms_and_programming_Practice_2019 (1).pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/question/page-008.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/markscheme/page-020.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/dev-qa/q-4-a-ii.png"}},{"question_id":"4(a)(iii)","parent_question":4,"topic":"authentication explanation","marks":2,"command_word":"explain","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Explain how authentication could be used as part of the defensive design considerations for this computer system."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark per bullet to max 2. Points: ensures only certain users can access the system; example of an authentication technique (e.g. using a password, biometrics, two-factor authentication).","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Ensures only certain (authorised) users can access the system","marks":1},{"criteria":"Example of an authentication technique (e.g. password, biometric, 2FA)","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/yearpapers/J27702_Computational_thinking_algorithms_and_programming_Practice_2019 (1).pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/question/page-008.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/markscheme/page-020.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/dev-qa/q-4-a-iii.png"}},{"question_id":"4(b)(i)","parent_question":4,"topic":"input validation algorithm","marks":5,"command_word":"write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The car dealership only sells cars that have fewer than 10 000 miles and are 5 years old or less."},{"type":"text","value":"Write an algorithm that will:"},{"type":"text","value":"- ask the user to enter the number of miles and the age of a car\\n- validate the input to check that only sensible values that are in the given range are entered\\n- output \`True\` if valid data has been entered or \`False\` if invalid data has been entered."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark per bullet to max 5. Points: miles and age input separately; checks for valid mileage (less than 10 000); checks for valid age (5 or less); checks both values are greater than / greater than or equal to zero (not negative); correctly outputs both True and False. BP2 and BP3 must check for both ends of the range — must check that input data is not negative. Example: miles = input(...); age = input(...); valid = True; if miles > 10000 or miles < 0 then valid = False; elseif age > 5 or age < 0 then valid = False; endif; print(valid).","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Miles and age input separately (two input statements, stored in sensible variables)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Checks for valid mileage (miles < 10000 or equivalent)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Checks for valid age (age <= 5 or equivalent)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Checks both values are >= 0 / not negative","marks":1},{"criteria":"Correctly outputs both True (when valid) and False (when invalid)","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/yearpapers/J27702_Computational_thinking_algorithms_and_programming_Practice_2019 (1).pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/question/page-008.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/markscheme/page-020.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/dev-qa/q-4-b-i.png"}},{"question_id":"4(b)(ii)","parent_question":4,"topic":"test data types","marks":3,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The validation routine from part (i) must be tested with normal, erroneous and boundary test data."},{"type":"text","value":"Identify suitable test data for each type of test."},{"type":"table","headers":["Test type","Miles","Age"],"rows":[["Normal","",""],["Erroneous","",""],["Boundary","",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark per row to max 3. Specific data must be given, not a description. Normal: miles in the range 0 to 9,999 and age in the range 0 to 5 (e.g. 7,000 and 3). Erroneous: miles less than 0 or greater than 9,999 and age less than 0 or greater than 5 / non-numeric data (e.g. 12,000 and 7). Boundary: miles of -1, 0, 9,999 or 10,000 and age of -1, 0, 5 or 6 (e.g. 10,000 and 5).","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Normal row: miles in 0-9,999 AND age in 0-5 (specific numeric values, e.g. 7,000 and 3)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Erroneous row: miles < 0 or > 9,999 AND age < 0 or > 5 (or non-numeric), e.g. 12,000 and 7","marks":1},{"criteria":"Boundary row: miles = -1 / 0 / 9,999 / 10,000 AND age = -1 / 0 / 5 / 6 (e.g. 10,000 and 5)","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/yearpapers/J27702_Computational_thinking_algorithms_and_programming_Practice_2019 (1).pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/question/page-008.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/markscheme/page-020.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/dev-qa/q-4-b-ii.png"}},{"question_id":"4(b)(iii)","parent_question":4,"topic":"iterative testing definition","marks":1,"command_word":"identify","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Identify when iterative testing is performed."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark. Accept: during development / whilst writing the program / before development is complete.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"During development / whilst writing the program / before development is complete","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/yearpapers/J27702_Computational_thinking_algorithms_and_programming_Practice_2019 (1).pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/question/page-008.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/markscheme/page-020.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/dev-qa/q-4-b-iii.png"}},{"question_id":"4(c)","parent_question":4,"topic":"battery charge algorithm","marks":6,"command_word":"write","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The car dealership sells electric cars, which require charging before they can be driven. Charging the battery by 1% takes 10 minutes."},{"type":"text","value":"For example, a battery has 80% charge. It would take 200 minutes, or 3 hours and 20 minutes to charge to 100%."},{"type":"text","value":"Write an algorithm that:"},{"type":"text","value":"- asks the user for the current battery charge percentage\\n- outputs \`\\"full\\"\` for a battery currently at 100%\\n- calculates how long this battery would take to charge\\n- outputs this in hours and minutes."}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark per bullet to max 6. Points: inputs the current battery charge percentage; outputs \\"full\\" if the charge is 100%; calculates the amount to charge (100 - current); calculates the time in minutes (amount * 10); converts to hours and minutes (e.g. using DIV and MOD, or repeated division); outputs the time in hours and minutes. Allow FT for the final bullet if a reasonable attempt at conversion has been made. Allow output of 0 hours 0 minutes if full. Example: charge = input(\\"enter battery charge\\"); if charge == 100 then print(\\"full\\") else time = (100-charge) * 10; hours = time DIV 60; mins = time MOD 60; print(hours, mins); endif.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"Inputs the current battery charge percentage and stores it in a variable","marks":1},{"criteria":"Outputs \\"full\\" when the charge is 100%","marks":1},{"criteria":"Calculates the amount still to charge (e.g. 100 - charge)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Calculates the time in minutes (amount to charge multiplied by 10)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Converts the time into hours and minutes (e.g. DIV 60 / MOD 60)","marks":1},{"criteria":"Outputs the time in hours and minutes","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/yearpapers/J27702_Computational_thinking_algorithms_and_programming_Practice_2019 (1).pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/question/page-009.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/markscheme/page-021.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/dev-qa/q-4-c.png"}},{"question_id":"5(a)","parent_question":5,"topic":"SQL query selection","marks":3,"command_word":"tick","content":[{"type":"text","value":"OCR Tech is an online shop that sells electronics such as TVs and game consoles."},{"type":"text","value":"Items for sale are stored in the database table \`tblStock\`. An extract of this table is shown."},{"type":"table","headers":["ItemCode","ItemName","Price (£)","Stock"],"rows":[["GSC5","GameStation5 console","249.99","102"],["TV4K","4K Television","499.99","18"],["ABRR","Audiobook reader","59.99","27"],["NAGC","TV streaming stick","24.99","192"]]},{"type":"text","value":"Tick one box in each section to identify the correct SQL statement to select the item code and item name for all items that have a price of £60 or over."},{"type":"table","headers":["SELECT clause","Tick one box"],"rows":[["SELECT ItemCode AND ItemName",""],["SELECT ItemCode, ItemName",""],["SELECT ItemCode & ItemName",""]]},{"type":"table","headers":["FROM clause","Tick one box"],"rows":[["FROM tblStock",""],["FROM table",""],["FROM database",""]]},{"type":"table","headers":["WHERE clause","Tick one box"],"rows":[["WHERE Price <= 60",""],["WHERE Price > 60",""],["WHERE Price >= 60",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark per correct choice. Correct statement: SELECT ItemCode, ItemName FROM tblStock WHERE Price >= 60.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"SELECT clause: SELECT ItemCode, ItemName","marks":1},{"criteria":"FROM clause: FROM tblStock","marks":1},{"criteria":"WHERE clause: WHERE Price >= 60","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/yearpapers/J27702_Computational_thinking_algorithms_and_programming_Practice_2019 (1).pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/question/page-011.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/markscheme/page-022.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/dev-qa/q-5-a.png"}},{"question_id":"5(b)(i)","parent_question":5,"topic":"test plan completion","marks":3,"command_word":"complete","content":[{"type":"text","value":"The following flowchart shows an algorithm to calculate the price of an item during a sale period."},{"type":"text","value":"[Refer to flowchart in paper — discounts £20 off if price > £100 and not > £200, or £40 off if price > £200.]"},{"type":"text","value":"Complete the following test plan for the algorithm."},{"type":"table","headers":["Price input","Test type","Expected price output"],"rows":[["50","Normal",""],["100","Boundary",""],["150","Normal",""],["200","Boundary",""],["250","Normal",""]]}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark if two of the expected outputs are correct; 2 marks if four are correct; 3 marks if all five are correct. Expected outputs: 50 → 50, 100 → 100, 150 → 130, 200 → 180, 250 → 210.","marks_breakdown":[{"criteria":"At least 2 expected outputs correct (from: 50→50, 100→100, 150→130, 200→180, 250→210)","marks":1},{"criteria":"At least 4 expected outputs correct","marks":1},{"criteria":"All 5 expected outputs correct (50, 100, 130, 180, 210)","marks":1}]},"maps":{"pdf-source":"data/pdf/j277/yearpapers/J27702_Computational_thinking_algorithms_and_programming_Practice_2019 (1).pdf","question-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/question/page-012.png"]],"markscheme-png":[["data/png/j277/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/markscheme/page-022.png"]],"dev-qa":"e2e/screenshots/yearpapers/paper-2-2019-practice/dev-qa/q-5-b-i.png"}},{"question_id":"5(b)(ii)","parent_question":5,"topic":"flowchart to code","marks":6,"command_word":"rewrite","content":[{"type":"text","value":"Rewrite the algorithm above."},{"type":"text","value":"You must use either:"},{"type":"text","value":"- OCR Exam Reference Language, or\\n- A high-level programming language that you have studied"}],"answer":{"guidance":"1 mark per bullet point to max 6. High-level programming language / OCR Exam R